4 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059591/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-43111" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "k=5"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059591/state.json" "file_sha256" "90e660673bc166676e860f53e1bf78c96bb1957df35ab66fba72bf7ee76dd251" "basis" 1914 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059591/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-43111" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "k=-6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059591/state.json" "file_sha256" "90e660673bc166676e860f53e1bf78c96bb1957df35ab66fba72bf7ee76dd251" "basis" 1914 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059591/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140043" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ be the unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6x+5y-3z=2 \\\\ 4x-5y+kz=1 \\\\ -2x+10y-5z=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that  $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $160-70k,$ find the value of $k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059591/state.json" "file_sha256" "90e660673bc166676e860f53e1bf78c96bb1957df35ab66fba72bf7ee76dd251" "basis" 1914 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059591/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140043" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's write the determinant of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}6 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -5 & k \\\\ -2 & 10 & -5\\end{aligned}|=160-70k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $x$ is given by $x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x}=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -5 & k \\\\ -2 & 10 & -5\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-5 & k \\\\ 10 & -5\\end{aligned}|-5\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & k \\\\ -2 & -5\\end{aligned}|+(-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 10\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(25-10k)-5(-5+2k)-3(10-10) \\\\  & =50-20k+25-10k+0 \\\\  & =75-30k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D}=\\frac{75-30k}{160-70k}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we know that $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, we can solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{75-30k}{160-70k} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2(75-30k) & =160-70k \\\\ 150-60k & =160-70k \\\\ 10k & =10 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059591/state.json" "file_sha256" "90e660673bc166676e860f53e1bf78c96bb1957df35ab66fba72bf7ee76dd251" "basis" 1914 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059591/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140043" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059591/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a1a6979836a56791792e28734dc7b6d21137040e0b92775cd68600d8dd8033e4" "basis" 1914 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059591/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140043" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/h