z=8 \\\\ -6x+4y+2z=3 \\\\ 5x-5y-15z=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, $x=\\dfrac{D_x}{D}$, where $D$ is the determinant of the coefficient matrix and $D_x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059591/example-6172.json" "file_sha256" "a709eb8c341aaea00a2a32028143a125b583ff9b6a1078376b4ab70e960dd497" "basis" 1914 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059591/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6172" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We are given the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}9x+3y+(-6)z=8 \\\\ (-6)x+4y+2z=3 \\\\ 5x+(-5)y+(-15)z=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059591/assets/00a69fe1d48b41e376971f3669ae51668733d36dfda18c0f9cc736d5d071a441.png)\n\nNow, to construct $D_x$, we replace the first column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059591/assets/14e850c9af7c0b2fdd1e36299667b2cbe91c84e8bb90030b729ee23b296ad984.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059591/example-6172.json" "file_sha256" "a709eb8c341aaea00a2a32028143a125b583ff9b6a1078376b4ab70e960dd497" "basis" 1914 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059591/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4577" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ be the solution of the system of equations below. Find $z.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y+3z=-1 \\\\ 4x-2z=1 \\\\ 3x+y-z=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059591/example-4577.json" "file_sha256" "333f3e960bcc067cf17472163594e62ac6d5414f0dbb528b9374219b4a7551e2" "basis" 1914 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059591/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4577" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's rewrite the system to reveal the coefficients:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1x-1y+3z=-1 \\\\ 4x+0y-2z=1 \\\\ 3x+1y-1z=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by finding the determinant of the coefficients matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|-(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}|+3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(0-(-2))+(-4+6)+3(4-0) \\\\  & =2+2+12 \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find $D_z,$ we take the coefficients matrix, replace the *third* column by the right-hand side of the system, and calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{z}=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|-(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(0-1)+(-8-3)-(4-0) \\\\  & =-1-11-4 \\\\  & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying Cramer's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{{D}_{z}}{D}=\\frac{-16}{16}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059591/example-4577.json" "file_sha256" "333f3e960bcc067cf17472163594e62ac6d5414f0dbb528b9374219b4a7551e2" "basis" 1914 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059591/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ be the solution of the system of equations below. Given that $x = 2,$ find the value of $k.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-z=-1 \\\\ kx+3y-2z=1 \\\\ 3x-y+2z=3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059591/example-4579.json" "file_sha256" "ea0bad510dc4dff86485f9480acabda17cb593eeb1e0b65e06a2da7233011cd4" "basis" 1914 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059591/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's find the determinant of the coefficients matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ k & 3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}k & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(6-2)-2(2k+6)-(-k-9) \\\\  & =4-4k-12+k+9 \\\\  & =1-3k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $x$ is given by $x = \\dfrac{D_x}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x}=|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(6-2)-2(2+6)-(-1-9) \\\\  & =-4-16+10 \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{D_x}{D} = \\dfrac{-10}{1-3k}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we know that $x=2.$ So, we can solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-10}{1-3k} & =2 \\\\ -10 & =2-6k \\\\ 6k & =12 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059591/example-4579.json" "file_sha256" "ea0bad510dc4dff86485f9480acabda17cb593eeb1e0b65e06a2da7233011cd4" "basis" 1914 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059591/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [] "usage" {"q-140043" [] "q-139985" [] "q-42681" [] "q-42693" [] "q-73690" [] "e-6172" [] "q-42671" [] "q-73686" [] "q-73698" [] "q-139987" [] "q-43124" [] "q-43113" [] "q-73688" [] "q-43111" [] "e-4577" [] "q-73694" [] "q-42623" [] "e-4579" []} "basis" 1920 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [17592186118678 17592186118679 17592186118680 17592186118681 17592186118682 17592186118690 17592186118691 17592186118692 17592186118693 17592186118694 17592186118786 17592186118787 17592186118788 17592186118789 17592186118790 17592186338565 17592186338566 17592186338567 17592186338568 17592186338569 17592186338572 17592186338573 17592186338574 17592186338575 17592186338576 17592186338579 17592186338580 17592186338581 17592186338582 17592186338583 17592186338585 17592186338586 17592186338587 17592186338588 17592186338589 17592186338592 17592186338593 17592186338594 17592186338595 17592186338596 17592186338599 17592186338600 17592186338601 17592186338602 17592186338603 17592186338606 17592186338607 17592186338608 17592186338609 17592186338610 17592186338613 17592186338614 17592186338615 17592186338616 17592186338617 17592186338620 17592186338621 17592186338622 17592186338623 17592186338624 17592186338627 17592186338628 17592186338629 17592186338630 17592186338631 17592186338634 17592186338635 17592186338636 17592186338637 17592186338638 17592186338641 17592186338642 17592186338643 17592186338644 17592186338645] "immutable_fields" [[17592186118677 :answer-field/correct] [17592186118677 :answer-field/id] [17592186118677 :answer-field/key] [17592186118677 :answer-field/type] [17592186118689 :answer-field/correct] [17592186118689 :answer-field/i